# Lists.
#
# Four primitives, and every consumer above them a fold that carries its
# answer as it goes: `listAt` gives an `Option of T` and `listLength` gives
# a bound, so walking a list is an ordinary function whose last act is to
# call itself.
#
# All four are primitives because they inspect or build a collection, and a
# collection is the one thing the language cannot make at a length its
# source does not know. That is the whole of the list layer's dependence on
# the platform.
#
# THE COST, PAID. Every fold below used to recurse once per element with
# no accumulator, so its stack depth was linear in the length of its input
# and four thousand elements ran the host out of stack rather than
# returning. Two changes closed that. §17.4.10's local binding gives a fold
# somewhere to keep its answer, so `sumFrom` and `joinFrom` now take what
# they have computed so far as a parameter and end with a call and nothing
# else; and the emitter turns a call in tail position into a jump, because
# no JavaScript engine does. Depth is now constant.
# `crates/zdc-codegen/tests/depth.rs` holds the numbers.
#
# WHAT §17.4.10 GOT WRONG. It predicted that local bindings plus a `rest
# of` would move `split`, `reverse`, `keys`, `values`, `listLength`,
# `listAt`, `join` and `listContains` into ZDeceptron. `join` and
# `listContains` were already written here. The other six did not move, for
# two separate reasons, and neither is fixed by a tail:
#
#   split, reverse, keys, values   still return a collection. `rest of`
#                                  makes a list SHORTER; nothing in the
#                                  language makes one longer. There is no
#                                  concatenation operator and no cons, so
#                                  a function that must hand back n
#                                  elements it did not receive still
#                                  cannot be written. The missing
#                                  construct is a way to GROW a list, not
#                                  a way to shrink one.
#   listLength, listAt             could be written now, over `rest of`,
#                                  and must not be. Both are O(1) on the
#                                  platform; written here each becomes
#                                  O(n), and `listAt` is what §17.4.3
#                                  dispatches `at` to — so every index in
#                                  every ZDeceptron program would go
#                                  linear to remove one `foreign`.
#
# The folds below keep their index for the same reason. `rest of` copies,
# so a fold that consumes with it is quadratic: measured against this file,
# `sumOf` over ten thousand elements takes 7.3s written with `rest of` and
# 88ms written with `listAt` and an accumulator. The tail is the language's
# operation on a list, and it is exported as one; it is not how a fold in
# this library walks.

foreign listLength is anywhere
    from "zd:list" as "length"
    takes of value is List of T
    gives Whole

foreign listAt is anywhere
    from "zd:list" as "at"
    takes value is List of T, index is Whole
    gives Option of T

foreign reverse is anywhere
    from "zd:list" as "reverse"
    takes of value is List of T
    gives List of T

# Everything but the first element, and `empty` when there is nothing to
# drop — so a fold that consumes with it needs no length check in front of
# it, only the `None` its `first of` gives back.
#
# A tail is a collection, so it is a primitive for the same reason
# `reverse` is. It is exported because taking a list apart is an operation
# a program wants and had no way to write; the header above says why the
# folds in this file do not use it.
foreign rest is anywhere
    from "zd:list" as "rest"
    takes of value is List of T
    gives List of T

function first of items
    give listAt with value is items, index is 0

function last of items
    give listAt with value is items, index is (listLength of items) - 1

function isEmpty of items
    give (listLength of items) is 0

# The target of `items contains value` when `items` is a List.
function listContains with items, value
    give listContainsFrom with items is items, value is value, index is 0

function listContainsFrom with items, value, index
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give no
        Some with item
            if item is value
                give yes
            give listContainsFrom with items is items, value is value, index is index + 1

function sumOf of numbers
    give sumFrom with numbers is numbers, index is 0, total is 0

function sumFrom with numbers, index, total
    when listAt with value is numbers, index is index
        None
            give total
        Some with value
            give sumFrom with numbers is numbers, index is index + 1, total is total + value

function join with parts, using
    give joinFrom with parts is parts, using is using, index is 0, joined is ""

function joinFrom with parts, using, index, joined
    when listAt with value is parts, index is index
        None
            give joined
        Some with piece
            if index is 0
                give joinFrom with parts is parts, using is using, index is 1, joined is piece
            give joinFrom with parts is parts, using is using, index is index + 1, joined is joined + using + piece
